  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe rest of today through Monday...\n\nConditions will remain breezy this afternoon under an upper low\nmoving across the region. Winds will decrease this evening. With\nthis trough, scattered showers (20-40% chance) may move west to \neast today, beginning over the west late afternoon and exiting \nacross the Dakota border late this evening. Over the highest peaks\nof the mountains, this precipitation will fall as snow this \nevening.\n\nWednesday and Thursday trend warmer and drier with continued\nbreezy conditions, strongest over the mountains and foothills west\nof Billings. Thursday looks to be the warmest day, with highs in\nthe 80s to low 90s. The combination of above normal temperatures,\nlow relative humidity values, and breezy conditions will bring \nelevated to critical fire weather conditions these days, \nespecially Thursday. A Fire Weather Watch has been issued for \nportions of the west Thursday afternoon and evening. Take care not\nto cause a spark!\n\nFriday into the weekend has more uncertainty as a system \napproaches. Models still disagree on the exact track and intensity\nof this system. What is certain is a trend towards cooler and\nwetter weather to end the week. Currently, highs look to decrease\nfrom mostly 70s Friday to mid 50s to mid 60s by Sunday. With these\nfalling temperatures, some locations may see their first frost (36\nF) over the weekend! The best chance is currently Monday morning,\nranging from 40-60% over the foothills to 10-40% over the plains.\nFor locations along the foothills, there is also a 5-20% chance \nfor a freeze (32 F) Monday morning. Mountain snowfall will also \nbe possible with this system, so continue to monitor the forecast,\nespecially if you have outdoor plans in the high country! Archer",
